// CORPORATE KOMBAT — BLUE CHIP — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// CORPORATE KOMBAT as a mid-80s corporate synth-funk / sophisti-pop record — a game alternate
// of office, selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces
// the live song.
// 
// Peter: "The bass style/sound for the disco mix might be good for the office cabinet theme.
// Can we add a new version of the office cabinet with that bass. Plus associated changes to
// make that cabinet sound more 80s?"
// 
// THE BASS is AISLE FEVER's: the fingered FRPP-6 bass on the bridge pickup (frppBassBridge),
// busy and syncopated, jumping octaves on the off-sixteenths, locked to the kick on the one and
// the three, a toneSine sub on every root (AISLE FEVER's gains and EQ). The line is new, on
// the office's beat-by-beat roots (G · B♭ · C · D in the hook); an FM pop (mrdrSlapPop)
// doubles its octaves from the first chorus. FLOOR 23 (29–36) is its feature.
// 
// The song is office.js as it plays now — 118 BPM, 60 bars, loop 5–52, the G-major lobby once
// (the cabinet screen loops 1–4 through the lobby-speaker PEQ) — the hook, the B riff, the
// counter-melody, the arp, the wind-down and the ending. Harmony
// coloured, not changed: Gm7 · Gm11/B♭ · Cm7 · Gm/D | B♭6 | Cm D; B: E♭add9 → E♭maj7 | D |
// Cm11 Gm/C Cm7 | D7; lobby G6 · C6 · D6 · Em7 · Cadd9 · Am7; the end on G6/9.
// 
// CALL AND ANSWER (v3, 24 Oct — Peter on v2: "the response is far too soft and muted… the
// 'Call' preset is also too harsh… attack in both not slow and levels match… I really want
// the back and forth to be a lot better. Maybe some overlap."). One pair: the CALL on a warm,
// fat Minimoog lead (bestClassicMono, 12 ms attack), the ANSWER on a funk horn section
// (mrdrHornStab, 6 ms attack) as lines (HORNS) and hits (HORN HITS), their levels matched by
// measurement. The call keeps the office's notes; the answer replies from the hook's tail and
// the two overlap (elision), tightening through the song into a hocket:
//   9–16   VERSE: Moog calls (office notes), horns answer after it — overlap a 16th–8th; the call re-enters over the answer's tail
//   17–24  CHORUS: Moog calls, horns answer from the e of two, under the call's last notes (2–3 sixteenths of overlap)
//   25–28  B: Moog the riff (office); horns answer on the offbeats — the E♭ run, the D chord a canon an eighth behind the call, the Cm run, the D7 section hits
//   29–36  FLOOR 23: Moog calls with hook fragments, the horn section answers with hits (and of 3, and of 4)
//   37–40  COUNTER: roles reversed — horns lead the office counter-melody, the Moog finishes each bar (a sixteenth or so of overlap)
//   41–44  B again, as 25–28, with the guitar chucking
//   45–52  CLIMAX: hocket — the hook traded note by note (45, 50), cell by cell (46, 49), the answer bar shared (47, 51); together on the turn, unison (48) then harmony (52)
//   57–60  ENDING: hocket, the turn together in harmony over the D7 hits, G6/9
// The FRPP-6 funk guitar (frppFunkMuted, left) chucks under the call — the & of 1, the e of 2,
// beat 4 and its a; the chorus adds the & of 2 and the e of 3 — strummed Alternate (up-strokes
// 4 dB under), top-four-string voicings, one note a string.
// 
//   1–4    LOBBY — DX piano bossa comp, vibes on the hook in G major, fretless bass, shaker,
//          CR-78 rim, conga, through the lobby speaker; the disco bass picks up into
//   5–8    DOORS OPEN — G minor: bass + sine, Eighties kit four on the floor, sidestick,
//          DX piano offbeats, orchestra hit, the hook teased on the DX bell
//   9–16   VERSE — call and response; the gated snare, PWM strings and guitars from 13
//   17–24  CHORUS — call and response over claps, sixteenth hats, open hats; FM pops on the bass
//   25–28  B — the riff, horn answers, orchestra hits on E♭ and D, riser, snare roll
//   29–36  FLOOR 23 — the bass's feature lines, crystal arp; the Moog's hook fragments
//          answered by horn hits; the pad back at 33
//   37–40  COUNTER — the office's counter-melody, the horns leading and the Moog finishing
//   41–44  B — again, the guitar chucking; the riser into
//   45–52  CLIMAX — the hocket, orchestra hits, Simmons into the loop at bar 5
//   53–56  WIND-DOWN — after the loop: E♭add9 Cm7 Am7♭5 D7, half-time bass
//   57–60  ENDING — the hocket once more, D7 horn hits, G6/9
// 
// 80s: the Eighties drum machine (kit_80s_pop), gated-reverb snare (snare80sGated → reverb →
// noise gate), claps on the backbeat, sidestick verses, Simmons toms, DX7 bell piano
// (mrdrDx7Keys), the Minimoog lead (bestClassicMono), a funk horn section (mrdrHornStab),
// Juno-style PWM strings (bestPwmStrings), sampler orchestra hit (mrdrOrchHit), crystal arp
// (tngrCrystalTrigger), the FRPP-6 funk guitar, chorus on the keys, pad and lead. No CRLS-1 voice.
// 
// Levels measured (solo LUFS through the master chain, 24 Oct): the horns sat 6–8 dB under the
// Moog at equal faders; now Moog / horn line / horn hits read −25.1 / −26.0 (verse and chorus),
// −25.0 / −26.0 / −24.4 (B), −25.7 / — / −26.3 (Floor 23), −25.9 / −26.4 (climax). Moog +1.5,
// horns +6 with a +2.3 Gain insert, hits +6 with +1.5; per-bar horn lifts in the arrangement
// (+3.5 in the hocket bars, +1.5 in the B answers).
// Trailing master gain -4.8 dB: the whole song reads −21.0 LUFS.
// 60 bars at 118, 122 s.
// Written by work/local/_office-remix-blue-chip.mjs through work/local/_remix-lib.mjs, which
// self-checks chord tones, semitone rubs, the guitar's strokes, the pair's attacks and the loop
// wrap; re-running it
// rewrites this file.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
